  "editorial": {
    "status": "working-edition",
    "notes": "The opening of the prayers at the foot of the altar: the sign of the cross and the antiphon, said before the psalm. It is a text of its own because the 1962 rubric divides the block exactly here — in Requiem Masses and, in Masses of the season, from Passion Sunday up to but not including Holy Saturday the psalm, the Glória Patri and the repetition of the antiphon are omitted, while In nómine Patris, Introíbo and Adiutórium are still said. What the rubric keeps and what it drops therefore cannot live in one document. The psalm follows as ordinarium.iudica-me, the versicle after it as ordinarium.adiutorium. Witness do prints a cross glyph inside the first sentence and handmissal-eo before it; the glyph is a direction for the hand, not a word, and is stripped as in the other ordinarium texts. FÍLII at w005 is the genitive singular, as the three-genitive formula requires. The Whitaker's port contradicts it — the port resolves filii as locative and plural only, reaching the genitive under the contracted spelling fili — though Whitaker's WORDS itself gives the genitive for filii first. Collatinus reads the form as we do. The divergence is a gap in the port, not a doubt about the case.",
    "source": {
      "method": "Verified by collation against the independent witnesses below: zero substantive divergence. Two accidental variants (punctuation, orthography) adjudicated in the apparatus.",
